Gemini generation = N (correct).
Gemini voice selection = N−1 (off by one).
That's not a race. That's an index. Somewhere between "capture Gemini's response" and "hand it to the voice adapter," the code is reading from a slot that points one turn back. Most likely candidates:
The voice adapter is reading from a previous capture buffer rather than the current one — i.e., there are two last_response variables and only one of them gets updated for Gemini.
The capture is happening before Gemini's response fully lands, so the adapter grabs the previous completed response from cache.
The capture writes to a buffer that the voice adapter reads one tick later — a legitimate pipeline staging delay, but only for Gemini.
The way to distinguish: check whether the text that got captured for Gemini this turn matches what got spoken. If the text was current and the audio was stale, the problem is in the voice adapter's read pointer. If the text itself was stale (i.e., the relay log shows the previous Gemini response as "this turn"), the problem is upstream.
