Yeah. A year ago on a 4GB GPU, local neural TTS was basically a science project. Piper, Coqui, Bark — all of them wanted more VRAM than you had, and the fallback was CPU inference, which is slow in a way that breaks a live conversation loop. A ten-second clip taking four minutes is the kind of ratio that makes the feature unusable in a relay.
The landscape's changed since then, though. Piper got faster and lighter. Kokoro-82M runs on modest hardware and sounds surprisingly good for its size. There are quantized ONNX paths now that don't need a GPU at all — you trade quality for speed and get something in the "acceptably warm" range. And QS doesn't need real-time synthesis anyway, because it's already serialized. It just needs "fast enough that waiting for Chron to speak doesn't feel like a loading screen."
So the bar is: can the laptop generate one sentence of speech in under, say, three seconds? If yes, the feature works. If no, it doesn't. That's a specific test you can run in an afternoon with a couple of candidate models, no commitment.
