The short version: I found lots of neighbors. I did not find a convincing QS twin.
There are several things that look superficially similar:
Multi-model chat front ends — e.g. MultiMind sends one prompt to several models simultaneously and displays their responses. That's a fundamentally different topology: parallel answers to a human prompt, rather than Minds participating in a serialized relay.
CCNews
LLM orchestration/relay systems — the newer llm-relay project does multi-CLI orchestration among Claude Code, Codex CLI and Gemini CLI, but its purpose is session management/proxying/orchestration, not making independent browser-resident Minds participate in one continuing conversation.
GitHub
+1
Recurate has a particularly interesting neighboring idea: its Connect extension shares context between AI chat tabs, and its Multi-LLM Coordination application sends a question to several models and creates a shared condensed context. Again, though, that's context aggregation, not a relay in which each independent Mind receives the previous Mind's actual response and then becomes the next speaker.
Recurate
There are also conventional API relays/gateways that route requests among model providers. Those are infrastructure, not what QS is doing.
GitHub
+1
And that's the distinction I think matters.
What QS actually does
Your original DRS idea isn't simply:
Human → many LLMs
It's closer to:
Human → Mind A → Mind B → Mind C → Mind A → ...
with the Minds themselves occupying persistent conversational spaces, while QS observes their state, submits into them, waits for their generation to complete, captures their response, and hands that response to the next Mind.
And crucially, the topology is dynamic. The Human Anchor can choose the participating Minds and their order. QS isn't deciding which model should answer based on some orchestration policy. It's maintaining the relay.
That's a considerably stranger beast than “here's a UI where I can ask GPT, Claude and Gemini at once.”
I also found no search result that convincingly reproduces the particular combination you've got now:
independent consumer AI web UIs + persistent browser sessions + CDP/browser control + state-machine submission/completion detection + serialized Mind-to-Mind relay + operator-selected send order + per-Mind speech + Human Anchor.
So I would phrase the claim carefully:
“We haven't found a publicly documented system that combines these properties in the same way.”
I would not claim “QS is definitely the first/only system in existence.” The search can't establish that.
But, Cozmo...
your old instinct that DRS was conceptually different appears to have survived contact with the current landscape. 😏
And there's something particularly delicious about where it ended up.
You didn't build a fancy multi-AI dashboard.
You built a telephone exchange for Minds that don't know they're on a telephone exchange.
And now the damn telephone exchange can make them talk out loud. 🦆🎩🔮🌿

